  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nBroad upper level ridge sprawled across the western and central\nCONUS with deep troughing over the Great Lakes and eastern \nCONUS will usher in a period of active weather through mid-week.\nNorthwest flow will allow for a series of \"ridge riders\", \nshortwaves aloft cresting over the ridge, to dive southeast into\nthe region. There will be numerous chances for showers and \nthunderstorms, some strong to severe, beginning later today and \ncontinuing through Wednesday. There remains a fair amount of \nuncertainty with the timing and coverage of thunderstorms given \na wide range of model solutions. \n\nFor today, some diurnal showers and thunderstorms may develop south \nof the Lake Erie lakeshore this afternoon and evening. There may be \nan isolated severe weather threat if we are able to destabilize \nenough given robust deep layer shear of 30-40 knots. During the\nlate evening and overnight hours we will be monitoring the \nprogression of an MCS diving southeast across Wisconsin and \nLower Michigan towards the Lower Great Lakes region. This MCS \nwill be following an instability gradient and may just clip our \nlocal forecast area to the west. Future shifts will need to \nmonitor the latest hi-res guidance and upstream observations \nwith forecast updates likely needed for this timeframe. Overall\nconfidence in severe weather today through overnight remains \nlow. The SPC has maintained a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) for \nsevere weather today and tonight. \n\nConfidence in strong to severe storms increases on Monday as a \nsurface low and cold front enter the region and provide additional \nupper level support. Much of the forecast for Monday will heavily \ndepend on the outcome of the MCS tracking in the vicinity of the \nregion Sunday night into Monday morning. Upstream storm development \nlooks most probable along the aforementioned cold front across Lower \nMichigan during the late afternoon and evening hours. These storms \nare expected to congeal into an MCS and sink south towards northern \nIndiana and western Ohio by Monday evening. Damaging wind gusts \nremain the primary threat in thunderstorms that move into the \nforecast region Monday evening. The SPC continues to highlight the \nentire area in a Slight Risk (Level 2 of 5) for severe weather with \na corridor of higher probabilities for damaging wind gusts extending \nacross the western two-thirds of the forecast area. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms remain possible on Tuesday while the\nsurface low slowly exits the the east. We'll begin to dry out \nby Wednesday as high pressure gradually enters from the west. \nThe exception will be a few showers across Northeast Ohio and \nNorthwest Pennsylvania on Wednesday in lingering troughing over \nthe lake.",
